package templates

import "github.com/ERaith/nightofathousandpixels/internal/web/viewmodel"

// MovieCardItem is one submission as a card, for a <ul class="card-grid">.
//
// The same card serves the slate and the "what you already have up" list on
// the submit page, because they are the same thing seen from two places. Two
// near-identical cards is how one of them quietly stops showing the trailer.
//
// THE SHAPE IS THE 2025 CARD, ported (nap-luo). That site's board read as a
// board and this one read as a list, and the difference was not colour:
//
//	title            the thing you are scanning for, so it goes first
//	badges           winner / withdrawn, before the quote rather than buried
//	description      a pull quote, because it is somebody talking
//	media            a FIXED 16:9 box that is always there
//	footer           a rule, then the credit, with room for the vote control
//
// The media box is the part that does the most work and the part most easily
// undone. It is present on every card whether or not there is anything to put
// in it, which is what stops a card with no trailer from collapsing to half
// the height of the one beside it. base.css reserves the box; nothing here
// measures anything.
//
// It is also never empty. A trailer that cannot be embedded -- the common case
// today, since E5 (nap-0z8) has not landed and board.movieCard leaves
// TrailerEmbedURL blank for every row -- puts its link INSIDE the box, so the
// box is the poster slot and pressing it is how you watch the thing. Only a
// film with no trailer at all gets a box that just says so.
//
// Every shape a movie row comes in is handled here rather than at the call
// sites: no year, no description, no trailer, a trailer that cannot be
// embedded, a blank submitter name, a title long enough to overflow the card.
// base.css does the last one -- .card__title sets overflow-wrap: anywhere and
// .card sets min-inline-size: 0 so a grid child is allowed to shrink -- which
// is why nothing here truncates a title. A film's name is not decoration.
//
// It takes the theme rather than reading one off the movie, because a card is
// rendered in two places — the slate and the submit page's "what you already
// have up" list — and both already know the page they are on. A MovieCard is
// one film; which season's theme it is being shown under is not a fact about
// the film.
templ MovieCardItem(t Theme, m MovieCard) {
	<li class="card">
		// Struck through rather than dropped: a withdrawn film that simply
		// vanishes from an admin list is how an admin comes to believe it is
		// still on the public slate.
		<h3 class="card__title">
			if m.Hidden {
				<s>{ m.TitleLine() }</s>
			} else {
				{ m.TitleLine() }
			}
		</h3>
		if m.IsWinner || m.Hidden {
			<p class="card__meta">
				if m.IsWinner {
					<span class="badge badge--accent">{ t.Text(viewmodel.KeyMovieWinnerBadge) }</span>
				}
				if m.Hidden {
					<span class="badge">{ t.Text(viewmodel.KeyMovieWithdrawnBadge) }</span>
				}
			</p>
		}
		// A pull quote, not a field. It is the one piece of writing on the card
		// that a person actually composed, and the left rule and the italic
		// are what make it read as somebody talking rather than as a column
		// out of a database. It is NOT set smaller: 2025 used text-sm here and
		// base.css floors body text at 16px, so this is one of the two places
		// the accessibility floor and the reference design genuinely disagree,
		// and the floor wins.
		if m.HasDescription() {
			<p class="card__quote">{ m.Description }</p>
		}
		// The media box, always. Three fillings:
		//
		//   a player, when the link has an embed form;
		//   the link itself, when it has not -- which is every link today,
		//     because E5 (nap-0z8) has not landed;
		//   the note that there is no trailer.
		//
		// loading="lazy" on the player is not a micro-optimisation. A full
		// slate is six of these, and six eager players on a phone on the group
		// chat's data plan is most of a megabyte before anybody has pressed
		// anything. The 16:9 box is reserved by base.css either way, so
		// nothing moves when they load.
		//
		// The comments for the other two branches are above this element
		// rather than inside it: a templ comment between an element's tags
		// emits its indentation as a text node, and the box's children are
		// sized against it.
		//
		// A link, never an empty player, is the rule for the second branch;
		// and the third says so rather than leaving a box that looks like it
		// failed to load. Six cards also means six links reading "Watch the
		// trailer" in a screen reader's link list, so each one carries the
		// film's name and the warning about the new tab.
		<div class="card__media">
			if m.CanEmbedTrailer() {
				<iframe
					src={ m.TrailerEmbedURL }
					title={ t.Text(viewmodel.KeyMovieTrailerTitle, viewmodel.PlaceholderTitle, m.Title) }
					loading="lazy"
					referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
					allow="accelerometer; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
					allowfullscreen
				></iframe>
			} else if m.HasTrailer() {
				<div class="card__media-fill">
					<a
						class="btn btn--ghost"
						href={ m.TrailerURL }
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						Watch the trailer
						<span class="visually-hidden">
							{ " for " + m.Title + " (opens in a new tab)" }
						</span>
					</a>
				</div>
			} else {
				<div class="card__media-fill">
					<p class="card__media-note mb-0">No trailer</p>
				</div>
			}
		</div>
		// The footer is a rule, the credit, and room for one control. Today
		// that control is the submitter's own way back into the film (ticket
		// E3); the vote control the 2025 card had lands in the same slot.
		//
		// A link and not a form: see MovieCard.EditHref for why a withdraw
		// button does not belong in a grid of cards.
		<div class="card__footer">
			<span class="card__by">{ t.Text(viewmodel.KeyMovieSubmittedBy, viewmodel.PlaceholderName, m.SubmitterLabel()) }</span>
			if m.HasOwnerControls() {
				<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ m.EditHref }>
					{ t.Text(viewmodel.KeyMovieEditCTA) }
					<span class="visually-hidden">{ " — " + m.Title }</span>
				</a>
			}
		</div>
	</li>
}
